Here is the fully updated, merged `gsplat_boundaries.glsl` file based exactly on his implementation:

```glsl
#[compute]
#version 460

#define BLOCK_SIZE (256)

layout (local_size_x = BLOCK_SIZE, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

layout (std430, set = 0, binding = 0) restrict readonly buffer Histograms {
    uint sort_buffer_size;
    uint histogram[];
};

layout (std430, set = 0, binding = 1) restrict readonly buffer SortBuffer {
    uint sort_buffer[];
};

layout (std430, set = 0, binding = 2) restrict writeonly buffer BoundsBuffer {
    uvec2 bounds_buffer[];
};

void main() {
    const uint id = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
    const uint count = sort_buffer_size;
    if (id >= count) return;

    const uint tile_id = sort_buffer[id] >> 16;

    // Handle the absolute first element
    if (id == 0) {
        bounds_buffer[tile_id].x = 0;
    } else {
        // Compare with the left neighbor directly from global memory
        const uint prev_tile_id = sort_buffer[id - 1] >> 16;
        if (prev_tile_id != tile_id) {
            bounds_buffer[prev_tile_id].y = id;
            bounds_buffer[tile_id].x = id;
        }
    }

    // Handle the absolute last element
    if (id == count - 1) {
        // End index is exclusive to match num_splats = bounds.y - bounds.x
        bounds_buffer[tile_id].y = count;
    }
}
```

### What Changed & Why His Version is Better:

**1. He Deleted the Shared Memory and Barriers**
* [cite_start]**Your old code:** You created a `shared uint local[BLOCK_SIZE+1]` array [cite: 267][cite_start], loaded the global memory into it, called `barrier()` [cite: 270][cite_start], and then did the neighbor comparison[cite: 271, 273]. This is a common compute shader trick, but it forces the GPU to synchronize all 256 threads in the workgroup.
* [cite_start]**His new code:** He deletes the shared memory entirely and just reads `sort_buffer[id - 1]` directly from global memory[cite: 279]. 
* **Why it works:** Modern GPUs have incredibly fast L1/L2 caches. Because thread 50 and thread 51 are reading adjacent memory addresses at the exact same time, the GPU caches the memory instantly. The shared memory overhead and `barrier()` synchronization were actually just slowing the shader down and introducing potential AMD-specific bugs!

**2. He Fixed the `id == 0` Bug**
* [cite_start]**Your old code:** `if (id >= sort_buffer_size || id == 0) return;`[cite: 268]. You explicitly skipped the first thread. You were relying on the `bounds_buffer` implicitly being zeroed out.
* [cite_start]**His new code:** He allows `id == 0` to run, and explicitly writes `bounds_buffer[tile_id].x = 0;`[cite: 279]. This guarantees the first tile gets its starting index recorded correctly even if the buffer has garbage data from a previous frame.

**3. He Fixed the "Last Tile" Bug (The one you guessed!)**
* [cite_start]**Your old code:** You had a hacky edge case: `if (tile_id == bounds_buffer.length() - 1)`[cite: 277]. This incorrectly assumed that the last splat in the array *always* belonged to the absolute bottom-right tile on the screen. If the bottom-right tile was empty space, the final splats in the list never got their `bounds.y` closed off, and they would vanish.
* *You actually left a comment in your code guessing the fix!* `//if (id == sort_buffer_size - 1) { bounds_buffer[tile_id].y = sort_buffer_size; [cite_start]} // instead??`[cite: 278].
* **His new code:** He uses your exact commented-out logic! `if (id == count - 1) { bounds_buffer[tile_id].y = count; [cite_start]}`[cite: 279]. This ensures that whichever tile contains the very last splat correctly caps its range.

