There is one major mathematical problem with generating a normal from a pure scale axis: An axis represents a line, not a direction. There is a 50% chance the pseudo-normal is pointing backward (into the object). If the sun hits a backward-facing normal, it renders pitch black.

To fix this, we implement **Double-Sided Lighting** in the compute shader so the splat is illuminated regardless of whether the normal is flipped.

Open **`gsplat_render.glsl`**, find the lighting calculations near the bottom of the loop, and change the `max(dot())` line to an `abs(dot())` line:

