The reason your lighting looks inverted (lit from the bottom) and the shadows are clipping (unlit rectangles on the edges) boils down to two brilliant graphics programming traps!

### 1. The "Normal Cancellation" Trap (Lit from the bottom)
Because a Gaussian splat is a symmetrical ellipsoid, the pseudo-normals you generated often point randomly *inward* as well as *outward*. When our compute shader alpha-blends 10 overlapping splats together, the inward and outward normals mathematically cancel each other out to `(0, 0, 0)`! To fix this, we must evaluate the normal in the Compute Shader and forcefully flip it to face the camera **before** it blends. 

### 2. The "Far Plane Shadow" Trap (Disregarded Depth & Unlit Edges)
Because we completely removed the `DEPTH` assignment in the last step, Godot defaulted to the physical depth of your Quad (which we pushed to the Far Clipping Plane). When Godot's Directional Light tried to calculate shadows, it thought your splats were an infinite wall 10 kilometers away. This causes Godot's shadow map cascades to slice through the screen arbitrarily, leaving 10% of the screen permanently blanketed in shadow!

We fix this by re-adding a **Depth Texture**, capturing the exact depth of the splats in the compute shader, and feeding it to Godot.

