You are completely right, and I see exactly why the last one failed! 

The problem was a pure math oversight on my part regarding the size of the QuadMesh subdivisions. 

If you set the QuadMesh subdivisions to 128x128, the physical distance between each vertex on the grid is `1.0 / 128.0 = 0.0078`. 
My previous shader used a tiny neighbor offset of `vec2(0.005)`. It was searching for neighbors *inside its own empty quad*, missing the splat entirely, and leaving the triangles horribly stretched! The "rectangles" you saw were the physical subdivision grid cells being warped to the far clipping plane, ruining the interpolated `VERTEX` position for Godot's Spotlight.

To fix this properly, we need a **Concentric Ring Search**. We will step outward based on the actual size of the grid cells to guarantee we find the silhouette and snap those background vertices forward.

Here is the bulletproof version. Replace your entire **`main.gdshader`** with this. 

*(Note: Because this search runs in the `vertex()` function rather than the `fragment()` function, it only runs on the 16,384 vertices of the quad grid instead of millions of pixels, making it extremely fast!)*

```glsl
shader_type spatial;
// CRITICAL: skip_vertex_transform prevents Godot from double-transforming our reconstructed VERTEX!
render_mode blend_mix, depth_draw_opaque, cull_disabled, skip_vertex_transform;

uniform sampler2D render_texture : source_color, filter_linear_mipmap_anisotropic;
uniform sampler2D normal_texture;
uniform sampler2D orm_texture;

// We define the size of one quad subdivision (Assuming 128x128 subdivision)
const float GRID_STEP = 1.0 / 128.0; 

vec3 srgb_to_linear(vec3 x) {
    vec3 higher = pow((x + 0.055) / 1.055, vec3(2.4));
    vec3 lower = x / 12.92;
	return mix(higher, lower, lessThan(x, vec3(0.04045)));
}

void vertex() {
	// 1. Grab the raw local coordinates of the quad [-1, 1]
	vec2 ndc_xy = VERTEX.xy;

	// 2. Convert to Screen UV to sample our textures
	vec2 screen_uv = ndc_xy * 0.5 + 0.5;

	// 3. Sample the splat depth from the compute shader
	float splat_depth = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv).a;

	// --- AGGRESSIVE EDGE DILATION FIX ---
	// If this vertex falls on the empty sky, search outwards in widening rings
	// to find the nearest splat and borrow its depth!
	if (splat_depth > 0.99) {
		float closest_depth = 1.0;

		// Search up to 4 grid blocks away
		for (int i = 1; i <= 4; i++) {
			float offset = GRID_STEP * float(i);

			// 8-tap search ring (Cardinals + Diagonals)
			float d1 = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv + vec2(offset, 0.0)).a;
			float d2 = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv - vec2(offset, 0.0)).a;
			float d3 = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv + vec2(0.0, offset)).a;
			float d4 = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv - vec2(0.0, offset)).a;
			float d5 = texture(normal_texture, screen_uv + vec2(offset, offset)).a;
