This is a great idea. Adapting your importer to his architecture is actually incredibly satisfying because his codebase uses a highly modular "Decoder -> Builder -> Resource" pipeline. 

Even better, his `GaussianResourceBuilder.build()` function expects the `canonical` dictionary to contain **linear scale**, **normalized opacities**, and standard Godot **Quaternions**[cite: 20]! Because Khronos's `KHR_gaussian_splatting` standard *also* uses linear scale, normalized opacity, and XYZW quaternions[cite: 11, 12], we do not have to do any of the complex inverse-sigmoid or inverse-log math we did for your implementation. The glTF data plugs into his canonical builder perfectly as-is.

I have strictly followed his coding style, including his `[gdgs]:` print prefixes[cite: 19], his dictionary-based error bubbling[cite: 20, 21], and his explicit `Resource` typing for the editor[cite: 19].

