That "rainbow/gay" color explosion is the classic signature of scrambled Spherical Harmonics! You just correctly deduced that there is a data transformation mismatch. 

I completely overlooked a crucial detail in how the Khronos standard packs data compared to the original `.ply` standard, and it completely scrambled the RGB channels!

### The Problem: RGB Interleaving
The Khronos glTF specification states: *"Each coefficient contains three values representing the red, green, and blue channels of the spherical harmonic"*[cite: 24]. It stores them as `VEC3` arrays, meaning the floats are physically interleaved in the file as `[R, G, B, R, G, B...]`[cite: 24].

However, the original `.ply` file format (which your `SplatCloudData.DEFAULT_PROPERTIES` and `splat_cloud_data_3.gd` expect) stores the SH data **separated by channel**. It expects the first 15 floats to be all the Red values, the next 15 to be all the Green values, and the last 15 to be all the Blue values.

My previous importer mapped the `[R, G, B]` glTF data straight into the PLY slots sequentially. As a result, your compute shader was reading Green values as Red, Blue values as Green, and applying wildly incorrect viewing-angle math to all of them, resulting in the chaotic rainbow colors!

### The Fix
We need to transpose the interleaved `[R, G, B]` glTF floats into the segregated PLY channels. 
`VEC3[0].R` goes to `f_rest_0`, `VEC3[0].G` goes to `f_rest_15`, and `VEC3[0].B` goes to `f_rest_30`.

